M-D19 template-release + review."}}},{"schema_version":"course-mode-generated-0.1","package_id":"slotframe-4b-009000","content_key":"apstat-4b-compare-009000","item_type":"mcq","difficulty":"Medium","exam_pack_ref":{"exam_code":"ap_statistics","cycle":"2026-27"},"taxonomy_refs":[{"scheme_key":"ap-statistics-2026-27","node_key":"unit-1"},{"scheme_key":"ap-statistics-2026-27","node_key":"topic-1.9"},{"scheme_key":"ap-statistics-skills","node_key":"skill-4.B","practice":4}],"cells":[{"topic":"1.9","skill":"4.B"}],"scenario_provenance":{"archetype":"Q2","archetype_label":"Multi-focus on Practices 3 & 4 (analyze data; interpret results)","task_verb":"Justify","practice":4,"modality":"exam_aligned_digital","validity_rules":["OBSERVATIONAL comparison only (no random assignment -> no causal claim)","means differ but spreads overlap so an over-strong claim is refutable"],"sources":[{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S5 (Free-response archetypes)","note":"Section II question archetypes Q1-Q4"},{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S6 (Task verbs)","note":"the item stem's verb must match the scored demand"},{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S7 (Response modality)","note":"digital Bluebook; text/numeric entry -> exam_aligned_digital"}],"domain":"biology"},"prompt":"In an observational garden study, plants in sunny spots had a mean recorded height of 60 cm (SD about 10) and plants in shaded spots had a mean of 55 cm (SD about 10). A student claims: \"plants in sunny spots always have a higher recorded height than plants in shaded spots.\" Which statement best justifies whether the data support this claim?","mcq_form":{"options":[{"text":"On average plants in sunny spots had a higher recorded height (mean 60 vs 55), so the data support a typical difference; but because both SDs are about 10, the distributions overlap substantially, so the data do not support a claim that plants in sunny spots are always higher.","correct":true,"misconception":null},{"text":"These results prove that plants in sunny spots will always outperform plants in shaded spots on recorded height in any future study or population.","correct":false,"misconception":"over_generalizes_beyond_data","misconception_source":{"tag":"over_generalizes_beyond_data","label":"Over-generalized beyond the data using definitive language","evidence":"documented_cr","ek_codes":[],"sources":[{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S10 'General exam-wide conventions'","note":"2025 CR Report: conclusions must use non-definitive language ('convincing evidence'), never 'proves'/'always'; and must not generalize beyond the studied population"}]}},{"text":"The claim is correct because plants in sunny spots clearly did better on recorded height.","correct":false,"misconception":"restates_claim_without_evidence","misconception_source":{"tag":"restates_claim_without_evidence","label":"Restated the claim without citing statistical evidence","evidence":"documented_cr","ek_codes":[],"sources":[{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S4/S6 (skill 4.B; task verbs) + S10 'General exam-wide conventions'","note":"a justification must reference the statistical evidence, not merely restate the claim"}]}},{"text":"Because plants in sunny spots had a higher mean recorded height, being in plants in sunny spots causes a higher recorded height.","correct":false,"misconception":"association_implies_causation","misconception_source":{"tag":"association_implies_causation","label":"Interpreted an observational association as causation","evidence":"documented_cr","ek_codes":["5.2.A.4"],"sources":[{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S10 Unit 5 (5.2)","note":"EK 5.2.A.4: correlation does not imply causation (stated explicitly)"},{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S10 Unit 1 (1.10-1.13)","note":"observational studies (no random assignment) do not support causal claims"}]}}]},"parts":[{"part_key":"part-a","prompt":"In an observational garden study, plants in sunny spots had a mean recorded height of 60 cm (SD about 10) and plants in shaded spots had a mean of 55 cm (SD about 10). A student claims: \"plants in sunny spots always have a higher recorded height than plants in shaded spots.\" Which statement best justifies whether the data support this claim?","response_modalities":["mcq"],"points":1,"criteria":[{"criterion_key":"part-a-criterion-1","points":1,"description":"Selects the justification that affirms the on-average difference while rejecting the over-strong 'always' claim due to overlap.","required_evidence":"Correct justification type: affirms_average_rejects_overstrong_via_overlap","deterministic_checks":[{"kind":"mcq_key","correct_type":"affirms_average_rejects_overstrong_via_overlap"}],"accepted_variants":[]}]}],"provenance":{"generator":"course_mode_stats_generator/slot_frames.py","frame_id":"FB-4B-COMPARE-01","params":{"scenario":"an observational garden study","mA":60,"mB":55,"sd":10},"seed":9000,"release_status":"unreleased_generated_pending_review","note":"Authored conceptual frame; correctness from authored justification taxonomy."},"_load":{"content_sha256":"3c9a8b5468fa2e3cba0cf8841b810e15835d2f21dbade4fcd242f6d56dc59f24","school_year":"2026-27","is_computational":false},"item_package_payload_self":{"schema_version":"course-mode-generated-0.1","package_id":"slotframe-4b-009000","content_key":"apstat-4b-compare-009000","item_type":"mcq","difficulty":"Medium","exam_pack_ref":{"exam_code":"ap_statistics","cycle":"2026-27"},"taxonomy_refs":[{"scheme_key":"ap-statistics-2026-27","node_key":"unit-1"},{"scheme_key":"ap-statistics-2026-27","node_key":"topic-1.9"},{"scheme_key":"ap-statistics-skills","node_key":"skill-4.B","practice":4}],"cells":[{"topic":"1.9","skill":"4.B"}],"scenario_provenance":{"archetype":"Q2","archetype_label":"Multi-focus on Practices 3 & 4 (analyze data; interpret re